  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.4 times its height. Along x a step multiplies x by (1 - 2.5 x rate), so rates above 0.8 make swings grow until the run diverges (loss above 5, where the ball reaches the bowl's x edge).",
    "Each step moves by -rate x (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with spread sqrt((64 - b) / (63 b)) for batch b: 1 at b = 1 and exactly 0 at 64. It comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 4 times their true length, one scale for both. The purple one is the mini-batch step (the way the ball moves, against the noisy slope); the dark one is the full-data step (against the true slope).",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 5 (diverged). The trail holds the start point plus one point per step; the kept ghost holds the same.",
    "A diverged ball and any path points off the bowl are drawn at the rim. The loss readout keeps the true value. The step-length readout shows only while a run is going.",
    "A single noisy step lowers the loss only on average; with small batches some steps raise it."
  ]
}